1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,702
Aarav presses start on the microwave.

2
00:00:02,702 --> 00:00:03,774
Nothing happens.

3
00:00:04,074 --> 00:00:05,371
The door is open.

4
00:00:05,371 --> 00:00:08,556
He shuts the door, presses
start, and it runs.

5
00:00:08,856 --> 00:00:10,604
Two conditions, one decision.

6
00:00:10,604 --> 00:00:11,504
Who decides?

7
00:00:11,435 --> 00:00:12,360
A logic gate.

8
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:15,149
Digital circuits use only two levels.

9
00:00:15,149 --> 00:00:16,247
One means high.

10
00:00:16,247 --> 00:00:17,345
Zero means low.

11
00:00:17,695 --> 00:00:21,184
A logic gate takes one or more
inputs, and gives one output.

12
00:00:21,534 --> 00:00:23,866
Its rule is written in a truth table.

13
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:26,234
It lists every input, and the output.

14
00:00:26,584 --> 00:00:29,032
With two inputs, there are four rows.

15
00:00:29,032 --> 00:00:31,816
Zero zero, zero one, one zero, one one.

16
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:34,115
Three inputs give eight rows.

17
00:00:34,115 --> 00:00:37,009
In general, n inputs give
two to the power n rows.

18
00:00:37,659 --> 00:00:39,136
First, the AND gate.

19
00:00:39,136 --> 00:00:42,536
Think of two switches in a
line, one after the other.

20
00:00:42,886 --> 00:00:46,460
Current flows only if switch A
and switch B are both closed.

21
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,627
Let's fill the truth
table on the breadboard.

22
00:00:49,627 --> 00:00:50,527
Both off.

23
00:00:50,416 --> 00:00:51,656
The lamp is off.

24
00:00:51,656 --> 00:00:52,556
Zero.

25
00:00:52,545 --> 00:00:53,732
A on, B off.

26
00:00:53,732 --> 00:00:54,632
Still off.

27
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:55,500
Zero.

28
00:00:55,509 --> 00:00:56,790
A off, B on.

29
00:00:56,790 --> 00:00:57,780
Zero again.

30
00:00:58,130 --> 00:00:59,030
Both on.

31
00:00:58,901 --> 00:01:00,341
The lamp lights up.

32
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:01,241
One.

33
00:01:01,201 --> 00:01:04,074
So the output is one only
when all the inputs are one.

34
00:01:04,424 --> 00:01:06,508
We write Y equals A dot B.

35
00:01:06,508 --> 00:01:07,978
The dot means AND.

36
00:01:08,628 --> 00:01:10,981
An AND gate can have three or more inputs.

37
00:01:10,981 --> 00:01:13,628
It still gives one only
when every input is one.

38
00:01:14,278 --> 00:01:15,682
Next, the OR gate.

39
00:01:15,682 --> 00:01:18,161
Now the two switches sit side by side.

40
00:01:18,511 --> 00:01:20,197
Current can take either path.

41
00:01:20,547 --> 00:01:21,447
Both off.

42
00:01:21,376 --> 00:01:22,276
Zero.

43
00:01:21,942 --> 00:01:22,842
A on.

44
00:01:22,568 --> 00:01:23,468
One.

45
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:23,983
B on.

46
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:24,610
One.

47
00:01:24,225 --> 00:01:25,125
Both on.

48
00:01:25,003 --> 00:01:25,903
One.

49
00:01:25,868 --> 00:01:29,083
So the output is one when
at least one input is one.

50
00:01:29,433 --> 00:01:31,407
We write Y equals A plus B.

51
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:32,961
Here, plus means OR.

52
00:01:32,961 --> 00:01:34,671
It does not mean adding.

53
00:01:35,321 --> 00:01:37,686
Think of a doorbell with two buttons.

54
00:01:37,686 --> 00:01:39,807
Press either one, and it rings.

55
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:40,727
That is OR.

56
00:01:41,377 --> 00:01:42,873
Last, the NOT gate.

57
00:01:42,873 --> 00:01:44,475
It has only one input.

58
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:47,091
Here the switch sits across the lamp.

59
00:01:47,091 --> 00:01:49,401
Open the switch, and the lamp glows.

60
00:01:49,751 --> 00:01:52,314
Close it, and the current skips the lamp.

61
00:01:52,314 --> 00:01:53,596
The lamp goes dark.

62
00:01:53,946 --> 00:01:55,705
So the NOT gate flips the input.

63
00:01:55,705 --> 00:01:56,697
Zero becomes one.

64
00:01:56,697 --> 00:01:57,688
One becomes zero.

65
00:01:58,038 --> 00:01:59,898
We write Y equals A bar.

66
00:01:59,898 --> 00:02:01,327
The bar means NOT.

67
00:02:01,327 --> 00:02:03,495
It is also called an inverter.

68
00:02:04,145 --> 00:02:06,432
A NOT gate always has just one input.

69
00:02:06,432 --> 00:02:08,498
AND and OR can have two or more.

70
00:02:09,148 --> 00:02:12,063
Now let's feed changing
inputs, and watch the scope.

71
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:14,238
Input A switches slowly.

72
00:02:14,238 --> 00:02:16,570
Input B switches twice as fast.

73
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:19,787
The AND output is high only
where both inputs are high.

74
00:02:20,137 --> 00:02:21,570
Swap in an OR gate.

75
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:24,914
Now the output is high
wherever either input is high.

76
00:02:25,564 --> 00:02:29,059
The gate follows its truth
table, moment by moment.

77
00:02:29,709 --> 00:02:30,774
AND needs all.

78
00:02:30,774 --> 00:02:31,792
OR needs any.

79
00:02:31,792 --> 00:02:32,692
NOT flips.

80
00:02:33,412 --> 00:02:34,432
Worked example.

81
00:02:34,432 --> 00:02:36,325
A and B go into an OR gate.

82
00:02:36,325 --> 00:02:37,927
C goes into a NOT gate.

83
00:02:38,277 --> 00:02:40,695
Both results go into an AND gate.

84
00:02:40,695 --> 00:02:43,291
So Y equals A plus B, dot C bar.

85
00:02:43,991 --> 00:02:47,438
Take A as one, B as zero, and C as zero.

86
00:02:48,138 --> 00:02:50,315
A plus B is one plus zero.

87
00:02:50,315 --> 00:02:51,453
That is one.

88
00:02:52,153 --> 00:02:53,759
C bar is NOT zero.

89
00:02:53,759 --> 00:02:54,900
That is one.

90
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,310
One AND one is one.

91
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:58,553
So Y is one.

92
00:02:59,203 --> 00:03:00,668
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:03:01,018 --> 00:03:03,155
Many students read A plus B as adding.

94
00:03:03,155 --> 00:03:04,789
So one plus one becomes two.

95
00:03:05,139 --> 00:03:06,985
In logic, there is no two.

96
00:03:06,985 --> 00:03:09,456
For an OR gate, one plus one is one.

97
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:11,788
Where is this asked?

98
00:03:11,788 --> 00:03:16,057
In PUC board papers, and
in ISC Computer Science.

99
00:03:16,407 --> 00:03:20,645
JEE Main and NEET papers have also
asked truth table questions on these

100
00:03:20,645 --> 00:03:21,545
gates.

101
00:03:21,558 --> 00:03:25,762
Expect gate symbols, truth tables,
and the output of small gate circuits.

102
00:03:26,412 --> 00:03:27,312
Try this.

103
00:03:27,183 --> 00:03:29,325
Y equals A dot B bar, plus C.

104
00:03:29,325 --> 00:03:32,295
In how many of the eight
input rows is Y one?

105
00:03:37,695 --> 00:03:38,595
C.

106
00:03:38,111 --> 00:03:39,011
Five rows.

107
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:41,843
When C is one, the OR output is one.

108
00:03:41,843 --> 00:03:43,274
That gives four rows.

109
00:03:43,624 --> 00:03:46,667
When C is zero, we need
A dot B bar to be one.

110
00:03:47,017 --> 00:03:49,530
That needs A as one and B as zero.

111
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:50,933
Just one more row.

112
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:52,842
Four plus one is five.

113
00:03:53,492 --> 00:03:55,038
Screenshot this card.

114
00:03:58,238 --> 00:03:59,759
Next, universal gates.

115
00:03:59,759 --> 00:04:02,548
Can one kind of gate
build every other gate?
